# Context

## Background
T063 (round 7, agent experience). Agents already read hub data through the
read-only sql MCP tool, so doctor was scoped to only what that tool cannot
answer: state tied to this binary (schema vs migrations, parser output
versions vs the parsers compiled in) or living outside the database (watch
checkpoints, device identity, pairing credential, coordination plane).

## Why This Way
- Doctor is read-only by oath. store.OpenReadOnly opens mode=ro with no
  writer, so nothing doctor does can migrate or repair; a behind-schema
  database is a warning whose remedy names the migrating command.
- The FTS5 integrity-check is an SQLite write statement: mode=ro and
  query_only both refuse it. With no rank argument it checks only index
  structure (every content drift passes); with a rank argument it demands
  a %_docsize row per content row, which this schema violates on purpose
  (text-less events stay unindexed). Resolved by running the rank form on
  a private in-memory snapshot (driver Backup API) after dropping the
  maintenance trigger and deleting text-less rows there.
- Stop detection re-decides ingestion's stop read-only: parse bound+1
  bytes at each pending checkpoint cursor, report only a StopError at the
  cursor (where ingest acknowledged). Stops deeper in pending bytes
  surface after the next pass advances the cursor onto them.

## Gotchas
- A :memory: snapshot pool must be single-connection and every statement
  must run through one checked-out *sql.Conn; ExecContext on the pool
  while the Conn is held blocks forever.
- SELECT rowid / count(*) on an external-content FTS5 table read the
  content table, never the index, so no SELECT-only integrity substitute
  exists.
- The coordination probe counts any HTTP response as answered (the
  control plane 404s on /); an unreachable plane warns, not errors.

